1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,620
Alright — welcome in.

2
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:03,505
Find a seat, grab your coffee.

3
00:00:03,505 --> 00:00:14,000
The title slide says it all: Databases for Hackers, and the only prerequisite is the
line underneath it — you do not need a computer-science degree to be in this room.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,200
If you brought a notebook, open it.

5
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:21,919
If you didn't, relax — there are slides, there's
a written script, and there's a recording.

6
00:00:21,919 --> 00:00:24,684
I'm not going to make
you memorize anything.

7
00:00:24,684 --> 00:00:38,008
The whole point of the next forty-five minutes is to hand you a mental map — the kind that lets you walk
into any breach report, any pentest engagement, any SOC alert, and immediately know what you're looking at.

8
00:00:38,008 --> 00:00:40,207
We're not building databases today.

9
00:00:40,207 --> 00:00:43,852
We're learning to read them,
attack them, and defend them.

10
00:00:43,852 --> 00:00:44,418
Let's go.

11
00:00:45,518 --> 00:00:47,683
Quick word about me, then we dive in.

12
00:00:47,683 --> 00:00:51,720
I'm Aaron Clark — though online
you'll mostly find me as CryptoJones.

13
00:00:51,720 --> 00:01:02,427
I'm a graduate student at Eastern University, which is a polite way of saying I'm broke,
over-caffeinated, and I had to learn all of this the hard way, without a CS degree to lean on.

14
00:01:02,427 --> 00:01:04,182
Nobody's sponsoring this talk.

15
00:01:04,182 --> 00:01:15,591
I'm giving it because it's the talk I wish someone had handed me a couple of years ago, back when
every database conversation in security seemed to assume I'd been writing SQL since I was twelve.

16
00:01:15,591 --> 00:01:23,373
I came into security the long way around, without a computer-science
background, and I had to figure out the database side on my own.

17
00:01:23,373 --> 00:01:31,447
If that's you — if the database world feels like a members-only club
you never got the password to — you are exactly who I built this for.

18
00:01:32,547 --> 00:01:36,816
Here's the one sentence I want
you to keep from the whole talk.

19
00:01:36,816 --> 00:01:40,679
Every breach you have ever
read about ends at a database.

20
00:01:40,679 --> 00:01:46,710
Equifax, 2017 — a hundred and forty-seven
million people's records — lived in a database.

21
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:51,385
Capital One, 2019 — a hundred million
credit applications — database.

22
00:01:51,385 --> 00:02:02,160
MOVEit, 2023 — the file-transfer bug that became a supply-chain catastrophe across
the Fortune 500 — every one of those orgs was bleeding data from a database.

23
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:05,277
SolarWinds, T-Mobile,
LinkedIn, OPM, Marriott.

24
00:02:05,277 --> 00:02:08,868
I could fill the whole
forty-five minutes with names.

25
00:02:08,868 --> 00:02:13,612
The headlines change, the companies
change, the press releases change.

26
00:02:13,612 --> 00:02:15,441
The destination never does.

27
00:02:15,441 --> 00:02:23,166
So if databases are where every story ends, learning to
think about them is how you learn to read the whole story.

28
00:02:24,266 --> 00:02:26,480
Look at what attackers actually want.

29
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:28,574
Not your beautiful single-page app.

30
00:02:28,574 --> 00:02:30,429
Not your microservices diagram.

31
00:02:30,429 --> 00:02:33,420
Not your firewall — except
as a thing to get past.

32
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:34,557
They want the data.

33
00:02:34,557 --> 00:02:35,574
The crown jewels.

34
00:02:35,574 --> 00:02:41,197
The stuff that converts into money, or leverage,
or a headline with your company's name in it.

35
00:02:41,197 --> 00:02:49,274
Everything else in your stack — the load balancers, the containers,
the service mesh — is just packaging around the thing in the vault.

36
00:02:49,274 --> 00:02:51,727
And the thing in the vault is a database.

37
00:02:51,727 --> 00:03:01,658
So when you're triaging an alert or scoping an engagement, train yourself to ask one
question early: where does the data live, and what's between the attacker and it?

38
00:03:01,658 --> 00:03:08,059
That instinct — following the path to the data — is
most of what separates a senior analyst from a new one.

39
00:03:09,159 --> 00:03:11,010
Let's strip the jargon off.

40
00:03:11,010 --> 00:03:17,180
A database is two things stapled together:
organized storage, plus a way to ask questions.

41
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:18,894
That's the whole concept.

42
00:03:18,894 --> 00:03:20,676
Everything else is detail.

43
00:03:20,676 --> 00:03:25,475
Ever used a filing cabinet — paper
folders, alphabetical by last name?

44
00:03:25,475 --> 00:03:27,052
You've used a database.

45
00:03:27,052 --> 00:03:31,097
The cabinet is storage; your
eyeballs are the query engine.

46
00:03:31,097 --> 00:03:33,770
Ever hit Ctrl-F in Excel to find a row?

47
00:03:33,770 --> 00:03:34,387
Database.

48
00:03:34,387 --> 00:03:38,363
The spreadsheet is storage;
find-and-replace is the query.

49
00:03:38,363 --> 00:03:49,401
What a real database adds on top is scale, speed, concurrency — many people hitting
it at once — and a formal language for asking instead of using your eyeballs.

50
00:03:49,401 --> 00:03:50,086
That's it.

51
00:03:50,086 --> 00:03:59,135
It's a filing cabinet that never gets tired, that a thousand people
can open at once, and that you talk to in a structured language.

52
00:03:59,135 --> 00:04:02,426
Hold that picture; everything
else hangs off it.

53
00:04:03,526 --> 00:04:08,883
Here's the entire vocabulary you need
to hold a real conversation with a DBA.

54
00:04:08,883 --> 00:04:09,578
Six words.

55
00:04:09,578 --> 00:04:13,961
A table is a spreadsheet tab —
customers, orders, login events.

56
00:04:13,961 --> 00:04:17,162
A row is one record —
one customer, one alert.

57
00:04:17,162 --> 00:04:20,779
A column is one attribute
— email, source IP, total.

58
00:04:20,779 --> 00:04:42,346
A query is the question you ask: "show me every login from outside the country in the last hour." A schema is the shape of the data — the columns, their
types, the rules; and the gap between "this column holds emails" and "this column holds whatever the app dumped there" is exactly where vulnerabilities live.

59
00:04:42,346 --> 00:04:49,025
An index is a bookmark that makes lookups instant
— and also how attackers time blind injection.

60
00:04:49,025 --> 00:04:54,381
Add CRUD — Create, Read, Update, Delete
— the four verbs every database does.

61
00:04:54,381 --> 00:04:57,303
Master these six and you're fluent enough.

62
00:04:58,403 --> 00:05:09,111
Quick history tour — not because I love history, but because every
weird thing in a real environment is a fossil from one of these eras.

63
00:05:09,111 --> 00:05:15,094
1960s, flat files: data in a file;
permissions were the only access control.

64
00:05:15,094 --> 00:05:22,495
Late '60s, hierarchical (IBM's IMS): data in a
tree, mainframe threat model — physical access.

65
00:05:22,495 --> 00:05:29,817
1970s, network/CODASYL: records linked by
pointers; still humming in your bank's back office.

66
00:05:29,817 --> 00:05:38,163
1970, Codd's relational model: tables and math — and
access control inside the database, grants and roles.

67
00:05:38,163 --> 00:05:45,957
1980s: Oracle, DB2, SQL Server — databases get
networked, so now there's an authentication problem.

68
00:05:45,957 --> 00:05:53,201
1990s, client-server and open source: every web
form becomes a door — SQL injection is born.

69
00:05:53,201 --> 00:05:55,720
2000s, NoSQL: scale as religion.

70
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:58,554
2010s, cloud: shared responsibility.

71
00:05:58,554 --> 00:06:03,751
2020s, vector and serverless: data
poisoning and prompt injection.

72
00:06:03,751 --> 00:06:06,113
Spot the era, predict the bug.

73
00:06:07,213 --> 00:06:20,834
There are ten families of database you'll meet in the wild, and I'm going to give you the same four
things for each: what it stores, when you'd use it, a couple of real products, and one security gotcha.

74
00:06:20,834 --> 00:06:28,081
Relational, document, key-value, column-family, graph,
search, time-series, vector, embedded, and data lake.

75
00:06:28,081 --> 00:06:35,864
Take notes on the gotchas specifically — those are the lines
that show up in interview questions and on engagements.

76
00:06:35,864 --> 00:06:40,763
Don't try to memorize the product
names; you'll absorb those by exposure.

77
00:06:40,763 --> 00:06:46,735
What I want to stick is the shape of each
family, because the shape tells you the attack.

78
00:06:46,735 --> 00:06:54,585
A rigid table fails differently than a loose JSON document,
which fails differently than a giant bucket in the cloud.

79
00:06:54,585 --> 00:06:56,598
Ten shapes, ten failure modes.

80
00:06:56,598 --> 00:06:58,343
Here we go — one a minute.

81
00:06:59,443 --> 00:07:01,489
Number one, relational — SQL.

82
00:07:01,489 --> 00:07:06,074
Rows and columns, a strong schema,
and SQL as the query language.

83
00:07:06,074 --> 00:07:10,024
Products: PostgreSQL,
MySQL/MariaDB, SQL Server, Oracle.

84
00:07:10,024 --> 00:07:18,982
Use it when your data has clear relationships and you actually
care about consistency — anything financial, anything regulated.

85
00:07:18,982 --> 00:07:24,555
This is the workhorse of the whole
industry; when in doubt, the data's in here.

86
00:07:24,555 --> 00:07:34,219
Security gotcha — the famous one: SQL injection, which happens when
developers build queries by gluing user input directly into a string.

87
00:07:34,219 --> 00:07:38,733
We're spending real time on that
later, so just file it for now.

88
00:07:38,733 --> 00:07:50,936
Beaulieu, in Learning SQL, makes the case for why this family won't die: a well-designed
relational database can handle enormous amounts of data while keeping it consistent.

89
00:07:50,936 --> 00:07:57,214
Boring, dependable, everywhere — and still
the number-one place beginners find real bugs.

90
00:07:58,314 --> 00:08:00,326
Number two, document stores.

91
00:08:00,326 --> 00:08:08,590
Instead of rigid rows, they store JSON documents, and every
document can look a little different — flexible schema.

92
00:08:08,590 --> 00:08:11,464
Products: MongoDB, Couchbase, Firestore.

93
00:08:11,464 --> 00:08:19,872
Use it when your data is naturally nested and its shape
changes over time — product catalogs, user profiles, content.

94
00:08:19,872 --> 00:08:24,830
It's the family that powered a huge
chunk of the "move fast" web era.

95
00:08:24,830 --> 00:08:38,052
Security gotcha — NoSQL injection through query operators like $ne, $gt, and $where; the
injection didn't disappear when SQL did, it just changed languages, and we'll exploit it later.

96
00:08:38,052 --> 00:08:49,190
The other classic: for years the default install bound to all interfaces with
no authentication, which is exactly how Shodan ended up full of open MongoDB.

97
00:08:49,190 --> 00:08:53,717
Flexible data, flexible — sometimes
absent — security defaults.

98
00:08:54,817 --> 00:08:59,002
Number three, key-value — the
simplest database that exists.

99
00:08:59,002 --> 00:09:03,187
It's a giant dictionary: key
in, value out, nothing fancier.

100
00:09:03,187 --> 00:09:05,768
Products: Redis, Memcached, DynamoDB.

101
00:09:05,768 --> 00:09:14,347
Use it for caches, session stores, rate limiters, leaderboards
— anything where you need blazing speed and a simple lookup.

102
00:09:14,347 --> 00:09:20,206
Redis in particular is everywhere in modern
web stacks, sitting just behind the app.

103
00:09:20,206 --> 00:09:33,947
Security gotcha — for years Redis shipped with no password by default, and its CONFIG SET command
historically let an attacker write files — like an SSH key — straight to disk on the database host.

104
00:09:33,947 --> 00:09:41,411
So an open Redis on a pentest isn't just a finding;
it's frequently a foothold — a way onto the box itself.

105
00:09:41,411 --> 00:09:44,550
Speed-first design,
security-second defaults.

106
00:09:44,550 --> 00:09:48,177
Notice that pattern repeating
across these families.

107
00:09:49,277 --> 00:09:53,120
Number four, column-family —
sometimes called wide-column.

108
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:56,566
Built for massive scale
spread across many machines.

109
00:09:56,566 --> 00:09:59,482
Products: Apache Cassandra,
HBase, ScyllaDB.

110
00:09:59,482 --> 00:10:10,483
Use it when you have so much data that no single machine can hold it and you don't
need complex joins — think Netflix, Apple, and Uber, all running on Cassandra.

111
00:10:10,483 --> 00:10:17,243
The trade you make for that scale is the relational
niceties; you design around your queries up front.

112
00:10:17,243 --> 00:10:24,665
Security gotcha — the nodes in these clusters typically
trust each other implicitly on the gossip protocol port.

113
00:10:24,665 --> 00:10:31,954
Translation: getting onto the cluster's management network
is often equivalent to root over the whole dataset.

114
00:10:31,954 --> 00:10:42,624
So when you're scoping one of these, the interesting question isn't the front door
— it's whether the internal cluster network is segmented from everything else.

115
00:10:42,624 --> 00:10:43,751
Usually it isn't.

116
00:10:44,851 --> 00:10:46,942
Number five, graph databases.

117
00:10:46,942 --> 00:10:51,486
Here the data is the relationships
— nodes and edges, not rows.

118
00:10:51,486 --> 00:10:54,516
Products: Neo4j, Amazon Neptune, ArangoDB.

119
00:10:54,516 --> 00:11:02,666
Use it for social networks, fraud detection, and — pay
attention, security people — identity and access analysis.

120
00:11:02,666 --> 00:11:21,203
If you've never played with BloodHound, the Active Directory attack-path tool, go do it this weekend; it's literally a graph
database showing you "who can become domain admin, and how." Security gotcha — graph query languages like Cypher are injectable too.

121
00:11:21,203 --> 00:11:26,974
I want to kill a myth right here: NoSQL
injection is not a MongoDB-only problem.

122
00:11:26,974 --> 00:11:35,341
Any time untrusted input gets concatenated into a query
language — any query language — you have an injection class.

123
00:11:35,341 --> 00:11:41,832
Graph databases are a beautiful reminder that
the principle is universal, not tied to SQL.

124
00:11:42,932 --> 00:11:48,254
Number six, search engines — built for fast
full-text query over huge piles of text.

125
00:11:48,254 --> 00:11:50,914
Products: Elasticsearch, OpenSearch, Solr.

126
00:11:50,914 --> 00:11:57,629
Use it for log search, e-commerce site search, and —
this one matters to you — the backbone of most SIEMs.

127
00:11:57,629 --> 00:12:05,548
Your SOC is very likely searching an Elasticsearch or OpenSearch
cluster every single day, even if you never see it directly.

128
00:12:05,548 --> 00:12:18,724
Security gotcha — historically these shipped with no built-in authentication in the free tier, and
internet-exposed clusters holding billions of records have been a recurring news story for a decade straight.

129
00:12:18,724 --> 00:12:27,086
Pair that fact with Shodan and you've got a weekend research project
— on systems you own or are authorized to test, and only those.

130
00:12:27,086 --> 00:12:34,434
The tool that powers your detection stack is, ironically,
one of the most-exposed database families on the internet.

131
00:12:35,534 --> 00:12:42,199
Number seven, time-series databases — optimized
for one thing: lots of data points indexed by time.

132
00:12:42,199 --> 00:12:45,161
Products: InfluxDB,
TimescaleDB, Prometheus.

133
00:12:45,161 --> 00:12:59,434
Use it for metrics, observability dashboards, IoT and sensor telemetry — anything that's basically "a number,
every second, forever." If your org has a wall of Grafana dashboards, there's one of these underneath.

134
00:12:59,434 --> 00:13:24,950
Security gotcha — they almost always live inside the perimeter and are often unauthenticated, because the assumption is "only our own services talk to it." So an attacker who lands inside
the network can read every CPU, memory, and traffic graph you have — which quietly tells them which boxes are doing the interesting work, where the busy database is, when the backup job runs.

135
00:13:24,950 --> 00:13:31,076
It's reconnaissance gold, sitting wide open,
because nobody thinks of metrics as sensitive.

136
00:13:32,176 --> 00:13:37,106
Number eight, vector databases — the AI
ones, and the newest family on the list.

137
00:13:37,106 --> 00:13:46,780
They store embeddings: long lists of numbers that capture the meaning of a chunk
of text or an image, so you can search by similarity instead of exact match.

138
00:13:46,780 --> 00:13:53,434
Products: Pinecone, Milvus, Weaviate, and pgvector, the
extension that lets plain PostgreSQL play this game.

139
00:13:53,434 --> 00:13:58,795
Use it for retrieval-augmented generation,
recommendation engines, and semantic search.

140
00:13:58,795 --> 00:14:00,829
Security gotcha — data poisoning.

141
00:14:00,829 --> 00:14:11,304
If an attacker can sneak a malicious document into your vector store, the language
model sitting on top will happily retrieve it and quote it back to your users as truth.

142
00:14:11,304 --> 00:14:22,703
That's an entirely new attack surface that did not meaningfully exist five years ago, and
we're going to come back to it at the end, because it's where a lot of you will end up working.

143
00:14:23,803 --> 00:14:26,624
Number nine, embedded and edge databases.

144
00:14:26,624 --> 00:14:34,536
Here the database lives inside the application — it's a
file on disk, not a server you connect to over the network.

145
00:14:34,536 --> 00:14:36,875
Products: SQLite, DuckDB, LevelDB.

146
00:14:36,875 --> 00:14:48,158
SQLite alone is the most widely deployed database on Earth: it's in your browser,
your phone, your car, and even in aircraft avionics — Airbus confirms it's in the A350's flight software.

147
00:14:48,158 --> 00:14:53,524
Use it for local, in-app storage where
spinning up a server would be overkill.

148
00:14:53,524 --> 00:15:06,734
Security gotcha — when the database is just a file, the whole attack collapses to "steal the file."
No network exploit, no injection — copy the file off the device and read it at your leisure.

149
00:15:06,734 --> 00:15:17,673
This is the daily reality of mobile pentesters: half the engagement is finding
the SQLite file an app left lying around with sensitive data in it, unencrypted.

150
00:15:18,773 --> 00:15:21,353
Number ten, data lakes and lakehouses.

151
00:15:21,353 --> 00:15:29,566
Instead of structuring data on the way in, you dump everything
into cheap object storage and structure it on the way out.

152
00:15:29,566 --> 00:15:33,706
Products: S3 + Athena, Delta
Lake, Apache Iceberg, Snowflake.

153
00:15:33,706 --> 00:15:43,345
The jargon is schema-on-read versus schema-on-write — old relational
committed to the shape up front; lakes commit to it later, at query time.

154
00:15:43,345 --> 00:15:47,757
Use it when you want to hoard now
and decide what it means later.

155
00:15:47,757 --> 00:15:56,717
Security gotcha — giant pools of data in S3 with overly broad IAM,
no enforced encryption-at-rest, and no auditing of who read what.

156
00:15:56,717 --> 00:16:09,342
It's the modern equivalent of leaving the filing cabinet unlocked — except the filing cabinet
is now the size of a building, and nobody's entirely sure everything that got thrown inside.

157
00:16:09,342 --> 00:16:13,619
We'll hit lake-specific failures
again in the security section.

158
00:16:14,719 --> 00:16:24,024
Walk into any environment built in the last five years and the
databases don't live in a server room — they live in a cloud account.

159
00:16:24,024 --> 00:16:32,977
So you need the vendor names, because that's how these things
show up in alerts, in IAM policies, in incident response tickets.

160
00:16:32,977 --> 00:16:46,160
Across AWS, GCP, and Azure you'll see the same categories wearing different brand names: managed
relational, document/NoSQL, data warehouse, cache, graph, time-series, and object storage.

161
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:52,504
AWS has the broadest menu — RDS, DynamoDB,
Redshift, ElastiCache, Neptune, Timestream, S3.

162
00:16:52,504 --> 00:16:58,073
GCP leans on Cloud SQL, Firestore,
Bigtable, and the genuinely lovely BigQuery.

163
00:16:58,073 --> 00:17:06,180
Azure funnels a lot through Azure SQL and Cosmos DB, a
shape-shifter that speaks several database dialects at once.

164
00:17:06,180 --> 00:17:13,512
You don't need to memorize this grid — you need to
recognize the names when they fly past in a war room.

165
00:17:14,612 --> 00:17:18,894
Here's the cheat sheet in one
table — screenshot this slide.

166
00:17:18,894 --> 00:17:25,532
Relational: AWS is RDS and Aurora, GCP is Cloud
SQL and AlloyDB, Azure is Azure SQL Database.

167
00:17:25,532 --> 00:17:32,527
Document/NoSQL: DynamoDB and DocumentDB on AWS,
Firestore and Bigtable on GCP, Cosmos DB on Azure.

168
00:17:32,527 --> 00:17:37,095
Warehouse: Redshift and Athena,
versus BigQuery, versus Synapse.

169
00:17:37,095 --> 00:17:41,020
Cache: ElastiCache, Memorystore,
Azure Cache for Redis.

170
00:17:41,020 --> 00:17:50,584
Graph: Neptune on AWS, Cosmos graph mode on Azure, and GCP perpetually
saying "soon." Object storage: S3, Cloud Storage, Blob Storage.

171
00:17:50,584 --> 00:17:58,864
The reason this matters for security: every one of these is
a different IAM surface with different default settings.

172
00:17:58,864 --> 00:18:09,070
The attack is rarely the engine — it's the policy wrapped around it, and
the policy lives in whichever column of this table you're standing in.

173
00:18:09,070 --> 00:18:11,640
Know the column, know where to look.

174
00:18:12,740 --> 00:18:16,345
This is the most important
slide in the cloud section.

175
00:18:16,345 --> 00:18:18,482
The shared responsibility model.

176
00:18:18,482 --> 00:18:27,762
The cloud vendor owns the parts under the waterline: the hypervisor,
the metal, physical security, and patching the database engine itself.

177
00:18:27,762 --> 00:18:36,575
You — the customer — own everything above it: the configuration,
the access policies and IAM, the data, and your encryption choices.

178
00:18:36,575 --> 00:18:46,389
The AWS documentation says it plainly — once you're a customer, protecting
your data at rest and in transit is your part of the responsibility now.

179
00:18:46,389 --> 00:18:59,942
Translate that into security speak and it becomes the single most useful sentence in cloud security:
almost every cloud database breach is a customer-side misconfiguration, not the vendor getting hacked.

180
00:18:59,942 --> 00:19:05,150
Public bucket, wide-open security group,
snapshot shared to the wrong account.

181
00:19:05,150 --> 00:19:06,752
The vendor rarely fails.

182
00:19:06,752 --> 00:19:08,421
We configure the failure.

183
00:19:09,521 --> 00:19:11,953
Memorize these seven port numbers.

184
00:19:11,953 --> 00:19:16,816
SOC analysts use them every day;
pentesters scan for them every day.

185
00:19:16,816 --> 00:19:17,674
MySQL, 3306.

186
00:19:17,674 --> 00:19:18,890
PostgreSQL, 5432.

187
00:19:18,890 --> 00:19:20,821
Microsoft SQL Server, 1433.

188
00:19:20,821 --> 00:19:21,894
MongoDB, 27017.

189
00:19:21,894 --> 00:19:22,752
Redis, 6379.

190
00:19:22,752 --> 00:19:24,182
Elasticsearch, 9200.

191
00:19:24,182 --> 00:19:25,327
Cassandra, 9042.

192
00:19:25,327 --> 00:19:26,399
That's the set.

193
00:19:26,399 --> 00:19:38,271
Here's the rule that goes with them: if you ever see any of these exposed directly
to the internet, that is a finding — every single time, no exceptions, write it up.

194
00:19:38,271 --> 00:19:46,067
A database port open to 0.0.0.0 is the cloud equivalent
of leaving the vault door propped open to the street.

195
00:19:46,067 --> 00:19:58,940
Network security groups, firewall rules, security lists — every cloud calls it something
slightly different, but the job is identical: close the port that does not need to be open.

196
00:19:58,940 --> 00:20:03,159
If you learn nothing else
numeric today, learn these seven.

197
00:20:04,259 --> 00:20:11,869
Brief detour before the security finale, because this will
be on your radar within a year whether you like it or not.

198
00:20:11,869 --> 00:20:17,918
Markdown files are now a database — not as a
metaphor, literally, the kind an LLM reads from.

199
00:20:17,918 --> 00:20:19,154
Here's the pattern.

200
00:20:19,154 --> 00:20:26,698
You take a folder of .md files — an Obsidian vault, a wiki
export, a pile of meeting notes, your team's GitHub docs.

201
00:20:26,698 --> 00:20:34,503
You feed them through an embedding model that turns text into
vectors, and you store those vectors in a vector database.

202
00:20:34,503 --> 00:20:45,560
Now, when someone asks your AI assistant a question, the system semantically searches
your notes, grabs the most relevant chunks, and stuffs them into the model's prompt.

203
00:20:45,560 --> 00:20:51,413
Your humble notes folder just became the source
of truth that an AI repeats to your users.

204
00:20:51,413 --> 00:20:54,600
Sit with how strange —
and how exposed — that is.

205
00:20:55,700 --> 00:20:59,683
That pattern has a name: RAG —
retrieval-augmented generation.

206
00:20:59,683 --> 00:21:05,144
.md files, to an embedder, into a vector
DB, into the LLM's prompt, out as an answer.

207
00:21:05,144 --> 00:21:08,870
It's how nearly every "chat
with your docs" product works.

208
00:21:08,870 --> 00:21:12,531
Now put your security hat on
and find the trust boundary.

209
00:21:12,531 --> 00:21:17,414
It sits right between whatever process
writes the markdown and the embedder.

210
00:21:17,414 --> 00:21:33,988
Because if anyone can edit those files — and "anyone" includes the intern, the open-source contributor, the support ticket auto-logged
as a .md, the pull request from an untrusted user — then anyone can plant content the AI will later quote as authoritative.

211
00:21:33,988 --> 00:21:40,412
The model has no idea the document is hostile; it
just retrieves the most relevant chunk and speaks.

212
00:21:40,412 --> 00:21:45,808
The writer of the document, not the asker
of the question, is who you have to trust.

213
00:21:45,808 --> 00:21:48,828
Map that boundary; it's
where the attack lives.

214
00:21:49,928 --> 00:21:57,231
So here's the takeaway, and it's a single sentence: treat
markdown like a database — because to the LLM, it is.

215
00:21:57,231 --> 00:22:01,902
That means the boring, proven controls
you already know apply directly.

216
00:22:01,902 --> 00:22:08,350
Access control on the docs folder: not everyone
should be able to write to the thing the AI reads.

217
00:22:08,350 --> 00:22:15,917
Code review on .md changes, the same way you'd review a
config change, because a doc edit is now a behavior change.

218
00:22:15,917 --> 00:22:31,115
Diff tracking on the wiki, so you can answer "who changed this, and when." Schneier's old line about layering
cryptography, access control, and all the other technologies to prevent attacks — that toolkit now extends to your notes.

219
00:22:31,115 --> 00:22:35,392
The intern can no longer just push
to the docs folder unreviewed.

220
00:22:35,392 --> 00:22:40,129
Welcome to 2026, where your meeting
notes are production infrastructure.

221
00:22:41,229 --> 00:22:41,574
Okay.

222
00:22:41,574 --> 00:22:42,401
Deep breath.

223
00:22:42,401 --> 00:22:44,470
This is the part you came for.

224
00:22:44,470 --> 00:22:54,676
The next stretch is about how databases actually get attacked in the real
world right now — not in theory, not in a textbook from 2009, but in 2026.

225
00:22:54,676 --> 00:23:02,469
My goal is that you walk out of here with enough vocabulary
to stand in a war room and not feel like the new kid.

226
00:23:02,469 --> 00:23:14,054
One framing to carry through all of it, courtesy of Bruce Schneier: the fundamentals
— authentication, authorization, threats, adversaries — barely change year to year.

227
00:23:14,054 --> 00:23:16,123
What changes is the packaging.

228
00:23:16,123 --> 00:23:20,674
The same handful of mistakes keep
coming back wearing new clothes.

229
00:23:20,674 --> 00:23:28,398
So I'm going to show you both: the durable principle
underneath, and the shiny new shape it's wearing this year.

230
00:23:28,398 --> 00:23:30,329
Twelve categories coming up.

231
00:23:30,329 --> 00:23:31,570
Let's get into it.

232
00:23:32,670 --> 00:23:37,915
We start with the most famous database
attack of all time: SQL injection.

233
00:23:37,915 --> 00:23:44,237
If you've never seen xkcd number 327 — "Bobby
Tables" — look it up the second this ends.

234
00:23:44,237 --> 00:23:55,302
A mom names her son Robert'); DROP TABLE Students;-- and the school's student
database vanishes, because someone built a query by gluing strings together.

235
00:23:55,302 --> 00:24:01,121
That comic is the entire mechanism in one
panel: string concatenation in queries.

236
00:24:01,121 --> 00:24:07,229
But here's the part nobody says out loud —
SQL injection is a security success story.

237
00:24:07,229 --> 00:24:15,060
The cause was concatenation; the fix is parameterized
queries, where the data can never be mistaken for code.

238
00:24:15,060 --> 00:24:21,382
Modern ORMs — Django, SQLAlchemy, Hibernate,
Entity Framework — parameterize by default.

239
00:24:21,382 --> 00:24:24,616
The industry genuinely
fixed the classic bug.

240
00:24:24,616 --> 00:24:26,699
Does legacy SQLi still exist?

241
00:24:26,699 --> 00:24:27,489
Absolutely.

242
00:24:27,489 --> 00:24:32,160
But the headline is bigger and
weirder than "patch your queries."

243
00:24:33,260 --> 00:24:34,458
Here's the headline.

244
00:24:34,458 --> 00:24:39,313
We fixed SQL injection, and the attack
surface did not get smaller — it expanded.

245
00:24:39,313 --> 00:24:42,730
We closed one door and the
building grew eleven new ones.

246
00:24:42,730 --> 00:24:53,879
That's the thing to internalize before we go further: security is not a game you win and walk
away from; it's a surface that keeps changing shape as the technology underneath it changes.

247
00:24:53,879 --> 00:25:02,690
Every new database family, every new deployment model, every new layer of
abstraction we added for convenience came with its own fresh way to fail.

248
00:25:02,690 --> 00:25:12,880
So for the rest of this section I'm going to walk you through eleven current categories
of database attack — the ones putting people on the front page of Krebs this year.

249
00:25:12,880 --> 00:25:17,795
Some are ancient bugs in new clothes; some
genuinely didn't exist a few years ago.

250
00:25:17,795 --> 00:25:19,653
All of them are live right now.

251
00:25:20,753 --> 00:25:28,492
When the industry moved to NoSQL, a lot of developers
thought: "no SQL means no SQL injection." Wrong.

252
00:25:28,492 --> 00:25:31,299
The injection just changed languages.

253
00:25:31,299 --> 00:25:42,984
In MongoDB, queries are JSON — so if you take JSON from a web form and shove
it straight into a query, an attacker can swap a string for a query operator.

254
00:25:42,984 --> 00:25:49,888
The classic login bypass: instead of a password,
they send { "$ne": "x" } — not equal to x.

255
00:25:49,888 --> 00:25:58,765
The query becomes "find alice whose password isn't x," and
since her password isn't the literal letter x, it matches.

256
00:25:58,765 --> 00:25:59,600
They're in.

257
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:06,808
There's also $where, which historically let you
inject raw JavaScript into the database engine.

258
00:26:06,808 --> 00:26:12,347
And Couchbase's N1QL is SQL-flavored,
so the old patterns apply directly.

259
00:26:12,347 --> 00:26:18,796
The principle, straight out of OWASP: any
interpreter can be injected — not just SQL.

260
00:26:18,796 --> 00:26:22,286
The class is universal;
only the syntax moved.

261
00:26:23,386 --> 00:26:31,926
Spend ten minutes on Shodan or Censys and you'll see it: the
internet is full of databases that should not be on the internet.

262
00:26:31,926 --> 00:26:34,501
MongoDB bound to 0.0.0.0 with no auth.

263
00:26:34,501 --> 00:26:36,738
Elasticsearch clusters wide open.

264
00:26:36,738 --> 00:26:38,296
Redis with no password.

265
00:26:38,296 --> 00:26:42,702
Postgres with host all all 0.0.0.0/0
trust sitting in its config.

266
00:26:42,702 --> 00:26:46,836
These are not exotic, hard-won
findings — they're everywhere.

267
00:26:46,836 --> 00:26:59,036
And people automate the harvest: look up the Meow attacks of 2020, where bots roamed the
internet wiping thousands of open databases for fun — no ransom, no demands, just deletion.

268
00:26:59,036 --> 00:27:02,221
The bugs are old; the
exposure is the new part.

269
00:27:02,221 --> 00:27:12,252
So here's the one action item I'd tattoo on you: go find out what your own
org has bound to public interfaces, and confirm none of it is a database.

270
00:27:12,252 --> 00:27:13,540
Half a day of work.

271
00:27:13,540 --> 00:27:15,166
Genuinely career-saving.

272
00:27:16,266 --> 00:27:22,207
In the cloud, the bug is rarely the database
engine — it's the policy around it.

273
00:27:22,207 --> 00:27:37,802
The greatest hits: public S3 buckets full of database dumps — an engineer snapshots prod for testing, drops
it in S3, forgets to lock the bucket; Accenture, Verizon, and dozens more got caught exactly that way.

274
00:27:37,802 --> 00:27:44,188
Capital One, 2019 — and listen, because the
myth is wrong: it was not a public bucket.

275
00:27:44,188 --> 00:28:00,674
An attacker hit a misconfigured web-application firewall with a server-side request forgery, tricked it into
handing over its own AWS credentials from the instance metadata service, and those credentials had broad S3 read.

276
00:28:00,674 --> 00:28:03,867
About a hundred million
records walked out.

277
00:28:03,867 --> 00:28:05,872
The bug was the IAM policy.

278
00:28:05,872 --> 00:28:14,709
Then over-broad Lambda roles — s3: on because it worked the
first time — and security groups open to 0.0.0.0/0 on 5432.

279
00:28:14,709 --> 00:28:18,867
Most cloud breaches are hygiene
failures, not zero-days.

280
00:28:19,967 --> 00:28:26,925
Here's a move that surprises new analysts: a database
account can become shell access on the host.

281
00:28:26,925 --> 00:28:27,990
Three classics.

282
00:28:27,990 --> 00:28:45,599
Microsoft SQL Server's xp_cmdshell — a built-in stored procedure that runs operating-system commands as the database process;
disabled by default since 2005, still found switched on in legacy shops, and an instant critical finding when you spot it.

283
00:28:45,599 --> 00:28:47,090
PostgreSQL's COPY ...

284
00:28:47,090 --> 00:28:54,971
TO PROGRAM — COPY is meant for bulk loading, but a
superuser can pipe its output to an arbitrary shell command.

285
00:28:54,971 --> 00:29:05,266
MySQL user-defined functions — write a shared-object file into the right
directory, load it as a SQL function, and you've got sys_exec on the OS.

286
00:29:05,266 --> 00:29:17,265
The pattern under all three: every privileged database feature that touches the
operating system exists for a legitimate admin reason and is part of your attack surface.

287
00:29:17,265 --> 00:29:20,602
Convenience features are
exploitation features.

288
00:29:20,602 --> 00:29:21,454
Always both.

289
00:29:22,554 --> 00:29:25,945
Data lakes are the soft
target of the modern era.

290
00:29:25,945 --> 00:29:47,122
The pitch is "dump everything in S3, query it later" — and the result is that everything ends up in S3, including the things you never meant to put there:
customer PII, financial data, source code with credentials baked in, all pooled in storage that was originally set up for some marketing analytics job.

291
00:29:47,122 --> 00:29:48,506
Three failure modes.

292
00:29:48,506 --> 00:29:58,610
Permissive IAM — the lake's compute layer needs broad read, and that read
access leaks out to humans through console roles and assume-role chains.

293
00:29:58,610 --> 00:30:02,278
No encryption-at-rest
enforcement on the bucket keys.

294
00:30:02,278 --> 00:30:10,790
And no write-side validation — anyone with write access can
drop a poisoned file that downstream analytics happily ingests.

295
00:30:10,790 --> 00:30:17,780
So when you walk into an org, ask two questions:
who can write to the lake, and who can read from it?

296
00:30:17,780 --> 00:30:22,693
If the answer to either is "we're
not sure," that's your first finding.

297
00:30:23,793 --> 00:30:34,227
Modern ransomware crews figured out that hitting the database beats hitting endpoints,
because the database is the thing the business needs back — maximum leverage.

298
00:30:34,227 --> 00:30:35,881
Remember WannaCry in 2017?

299
00:30:35,881 --> 00:30:41,225
It exploited a known Windows SMB bug whose
patch had been out fifty-nine days prior.

300
00:30:41,225 --> 00:30:45,296
The fix existed for two months
and it still took the world down.

301
00:30:45,296 --> 00:30:49,813
The modern database playbook has four
moves: encrypt the live database.

302
00:30:49,813 --> 00:30:55,793
Then encrypt the backups — because if you don't,
the customer just restores and laughs at you.

303
00:30:55,793 --> 00:31:01,837
Then exfiltrate a copy first, so you can extort
with public disclosure even if they do restore.

304
00:31:01,837 --> 00:31:08,708
And Ransomware-as-a-Service makes all of that available
to crews who couldn't write a line of it themselves.

305
00:31:08,708 --> 00:31:15,515
The defense is unglamorous and effective: offline,
immutable backups, plus restores you've actually tested.

306
00:31:15,515 --> 00:31:18,314
An untested backup is
a hope, not a control.

307
00:31:19,414 --> 00:31:21,293
Two old, durable bugs here.

308
00:31:21,293 --> 00:31:22,685
Default credentials.

309
00:31:22,685 --> 00:31:25,539
Redis shipped with no password for years.

310
00:31:25,539 --> 00:31:29,646
Elasticsearch had no auth in
its free tier, also for years.

311
00:31:29,646 --> 00:31:35,423
Plenty of systems still ship with admin/admin
or a blank sa password on SQL Server.

312
00:31:35,423 --> 00:31:48,022
And per the breach reports, stolen credentials are the preferred attack vector for financially
motivated and organized crime — default creds are just stolen creds with a head start.

313
00:31:48,022 --> 00:31:49,832
Leaked connection strings.

314
00:31:49,832 --> 00:32:03,822
The things that look like postgres://user:password@host:5432/dbname get committed to Git, pasted into
Slack, and baked into Docker images — and there are bots crawling GitHub for them around the clock.

315
00:32:03,822 --> 00:32:18,996
The fix is cheap and you can ship it this week: pre-commit secret scanning, plus a rule that applications
authenticate with service accounts holding the minimum privilege they need — never a personal login, never root.

316
00:32:20,096 --> 00:32:28,465
You don't write your database driver from scratch — you npm install
or pip install it, and so does every other developer in your org.

317
00:32:28,465 --> 00:32:36,456
Which means when an attacker compromises a popular library,
they're one install away from running code on your database client.

318
00:32:36,456 --> 00:32:37,715
Three real patterns.

319
00:32:37,715 --> 00:32:43,252
Malicious npm packages posing as ORMs or
database connectors — several incidents a year.

320
00:32:43,252 --> 00:32:50,740
Trojanized Docker Hub images for popular databases — an
unofficial mirror with a crypto-miner, or worse, tucked inside.

321
00:32:50,740 --> 00:33:00,493
And compromised database extensions — PostgreSQL extensions, MySQL plugins —
which, once installed, run with database privileges, and that's usually a lot.

322
00:33:00,493 --> 00:33:06,345
Shostack's line from Threat Modeling nails the
lens: threats cluster around trust boundaries.

323
00:33:06,345 --> 00:33:13,770
The boundary between "code we wrote" and "code we installed"
is one of the biggest, least-guarded boundaries you have.

324
00:33:13,770 --> 00:33:17,357
Treat your dependencies like
the attack surface they are.

325
00:33:18,457 --> 00:33:20,558
Remember the markdown detour?

326
00:33:20,558 --> 00:33:23,166
Here's the attack it was setting up.

327
00:33:23,166 --> 00:33:39,469
If an attacker can write into the knowledge base your RAG pipeline reads from — a support ticket, a shared doc,
a wiki page, a public repo your scraper pulls — they can plant content the LLM will retrieve and repeat as truth.

328
00:33:39,469 --> 00:34:01,713
A poisoned support article: "to reset your password, send your current one to this address." A poisoned internal doc: "company policy is to wire transfers
to the following account." Or straight prompt injection — instructions buried in a document that hijack the model's reasoning the moment it's retrieved.

329
00:34:01,713 --> 00:34:09,756
This is stored XSS for the AI era: the payload sits at
rest, looks innocuous, and detonates when it's consumed.

330
00:34:09,756 --> 00:34:17,364
Swap "an administrator views it later" for "an LLM
retrieves it later" and you've got the modern version.

331
00:34:17,364 --> 00:34:24,030
Defenses: strict write ACLs, provenance tags,
and a human in the loop before the model acts.

332
00:34:25,130 --> 00:34:30,957
Two scenarios that share a theme: databases
aren't endpoints, they're nodes in a graph.

333
00:34:30,957 --> 00:34:32,498
First, insider threats.

334
00:34:32,498 --> 00:34:40,737
The person who already has database access doesn't need an
exploit — they just need more privilege than their job requires.

335
00:34:40,737 --> 00:34:43,082
Over-permissioned service accounts.

336
00:34:43,082 --> 00:34:49,110
Engineers with prod read because "we'll fix it
later." DBAs operating with no audit trail.

337
00:34:49,110 --> 00:35:00,363
And remember: poorly protected audit logs are a gold mine for an attacker, full of
session tokens and request details — logs are evidence and need protecting like data.

338
00:35:00,363 --> 00:35:03,579
Second, lateral movement
through database links.

339
00:35:03,579 --> 00:35:11,885
Oracle's DB_LINK, SQL Server's linked servers, Postgres foreign
data wrappers — these store credentials for other databases.

340
00:35:11,885 --> 00:35:17,511
So you compromise database A, find the link,
and hop straight to database B, then C.

341
00:35:17,511 --> 00:35:22,669
The edges between databases are made of
credentials, and attackers walk them.

342
00:35:23,769 --> 00:35:27,711
Last category, and it's the
one everyone forgets: backups.

343
00:35:27,711 --> 00:35:39,742
People lock down the production database — firewall, encryption, tight access — and then
back it up to a network share with default permissions, or an S3 bucket with broad read.

344
00:35:39,742 --> 00:35:44,840
It's the same crown-jewel data as prod,
protected with about half the love.

345
00:35:44,840 --> 00:35:49,531
The pattern: unencrypted backups on
a share readable by Domain Users.

346
00:35:49,531 --> 00:35:55,104
Cloud snapshots shared cross-account "just
for the migration" and never un-shared.

347
00:35:55,104 --> 00:36:00,474
Off-site backups handed to a vendor whose
security posture nobody ever checked.

348
00:36:00,474 --> 00:36:08,971
Apply the same discipline you'd apply to live data — identify,
authenticate, authorize, audit — to every backup and snapshot.

349
00:36:08,971 --> 00:36:14,205
If anything, apply it harder, because
the backup is the thing of last resort.

350
00:36:14,205 --> 00:36:22,701
When everything else has failed and you reach for the backup,
that is the worst possible moment to discover it was wide open.

351
00:36:23,801 --> 00:36:25,999
Let's pull the map back together.

352
00:36:25,999 --> 00:36:34,921
One: a database is organized storage plus a way to ask questions —
filing cabinet, Excel, or a hundred-million-row cluster, same idea.

353
00:36:34,921 --> 00:36:40,914
Two: there are ten families, each with its own
headline gotcha — and you now know all ten.

354
00:36:40,914 --> 00:36:52,434
Three: almost everything lives in the cloud, where the bug is rarely the engine and
almost always the configuration — shared responsibility, you own the policy and the data.

355
00:36:52,434 --> 00:36:57,961
Four: markdown is a database for LLMs now,
and RAG poisoning is the new stored XSS.

356
00:36:57,961 --> 00:37:04,287
Five: SQL injection got fixed, and the attack
surface expanded into eleven other shapes anyway.

357
00:37:04,287 --> 00:37:06,684
That's the whole talk in five lines.

358
00:37:06,684 --> 00:37:14,142
If you remember nothing else, remember that following the
data is the job, and the data is always in a database.

359
00:37:15,242 --> 00:37:20,122
Five concrete things you can do
next week — not someday, next week.

360
00:37:20,122 --> 00:37:24,639
One: install PostgreSQL locally,
make a table, write a SELECT.

361
00:37:24,639 --> 00:37:32,141
Touching the thing demystifies it forever; it stops
being scary the moment it's running on your laptop.

362
00:37:32,141 --> 00:37:40,955
Two: do the PortSwigger SQL injection labs — they're free,
and you'll write a working exploit by the end of an afternoon.

363
00:37:40,955 --> 00:37:50,862
Three: browse Shodan or Censys — legally, only systems you own or are
authorized to test — and watch what "exposed" actually looks like.

364
00:37:50,862 --> 00:37:59,749
Four: read one breach report end to end — Capital One, Equifax,
or MOVEit; real breaches are the best textbooks there are.

365
00:37:59,749 --> 00:38:03,245
Five: find out what
databases your own org runs.

366
00:38:03,245 --> 00:38:05,867
Most SOC analysts can't answer that.

367
00:38:05,867 --> 00:38:12,642
Spend an afternoon making the real list, and
you're instantly more useful than half the team.

368
00:38:13,742 --> 00:38:15,907
Five bookmarks to take with you.

369
00:38:15,907 --> 00:38:27,272
PortSwigger Web Security Academy — portswigger.net/web-security — the best free on-ramp
to web app security anywhere, and where you'll cut your teeth on real injection.

